WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:07.071
A college must timetable six exams: algebra

2
00:00:07.071 --> 00:00:10.295
biology, chemistry, data science

3
00:00:10.295 --> 00:00:12.381
economics and French.

4
00:00:12.475 --> 00:00:15.604
Two exams clash when some student

5
00:00:15.699 --> 00:00:17.500
takes both of them.

6
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.014
Student one takes algebra

7
00:00:20.014 --> 00:00:21.929
biology and chemistry

8
00:00:21.929 --> 00:00:25.009
so those three exams clash in pairs.

9
00:00:25.009 --> 00:00:28.672
Student two takes biology and data science.

10
00:00:28.672 --> 00:00:31.253
Student three takes chemistry

11
00:00:31.253 --> 00:00:33.584
data science and economics.

12
00:00:33.584 --> 00:00:37.330
And student four takes economics and French.

13
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.073
Draw each exam as a dot and each clash

14
00:00:42.189 --> 00:00:45.310
as a line between two dots.

15
00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:48.200
That picture is a graph.

16
00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.058
Now count the lines at each dot

17
00:00:51.058 --> 00:00:53.175
and add up the six counts.

18
00:00:53.175 --> 00:00:55.135
Will the total be eight

19
00:00:55.135 --> 00:00:56.781
the number of lines?

20
00:00:56.781 --> 00:00:58.270
Pause and predict.

21
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:04.270
Here is the definition.

22
00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:07.426
A graph G is a pair: V and E.

23
00:01:07.426 --> 00:01:11.845
V is a nonempty finite set, the vertices

24
00:01:11.845 --> 00:01:14.054
here, the six exams.

25
00:01:14.054 --> 00:01:18.263
E is a set of two-element subsets of V

26
00:01:18.263 --> 00:01:22.050
the edges: here, the eight clashes.

27
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.623
The number of vertices is

28
00:01:24.712 --> 00:01:28.003
the order of the graph: n equals six.

29
00:01:28.003 --> 00:01:30.937
The number of edges is its size

30
00:01:30.937 --> 00:01:32.360
m equals eight.

31
00:01:32.449 --> 00:01:35.206
The edge with endpoints a and b

32
00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:37.963
is the set containing a and b

33
00:01:37.963 --> 00:01:40.720
and we write it simply as a b.

34
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.290
A drawing is only a picture

35
00:01:43.372 --> 00:01:45.017
of the pair V and E.

36
00:01:45.017 --> 00:01:47.154
Turn it or move the dots

37
00:01:47.154 --> 00:01:49.786
and it is still the same graph

38
00:01:49.786 --> 00:01:52.910
as long as the same pairs are joined.

39
00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.915
Two things are not allowed.

40
00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:59.329
A loop, from a vertex back to itself

41
00:01:59.329 --> 00:02:01.395
would be the set f, f

42
00:02:01.395 --> 00:02:04.629
which is just the set with f alone.

43
00:02:04.629 --> 00:02:07.234
It has one element, not two

44
00:02:07.234 --> 00:02:09.210
so it is not an edge.

45
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.913
And a second line between a

46
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.963
and b would be the set a, b again.

47
00:02:14.963 --> 00:02:16.358
But E is a set

48
00:02:16.358 --> 00:02:19.582
and a set holds each pair only once.

49
00:02:19.582 --> 00:02:21.151
Graphs like ours

50
00:02:21.151 --> 00:02:24.375
with no loops and no multiple edges

51
00:02:24.375 --> 00:02:26.641
are called simple graphs.

52
00:02:26.641 --> 00:02:30.040
In this course, every graph is simple.

53
00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.091
Two vertices are adjacent

54
00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:35.463
when they form an edge.

55
00:02:35.463 --> 00:02:39.713
Chemistry is adjacent to algebra, biology

56
00:02:39.713 --> 00:02:42.481
data science and economics.

57
00:02:42.481 --> 00:02:46.039
That set is the neighbourhood of c

58
00:02:46.039 --> 00:02:47.620
written N of c.

59
00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:50.327
The degree of a vertex is the

60
00:02:50.408 --> 00:02:52.196
number of edges at it

61
00:02:52.196 --> 00:02:55.447
which is the size of its neighbourhood.

62
00:02:55.447 --> 00:02:57.722
So the degree of c is four.

63
00:02:57.722 --> 00:03:01.136
French has a single neighbour, economics

64
00:03:01.136 --> 00:03:03.330
so the degree of f is one.

65
00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:09.105
Here are all six degrees: two, three, four

66
00:03:09.105 --> 00:03:11.880
three, three and one.

67
00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:15.820
The largest degree is called

68
00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:20.260
capital delta of G: here four, at c.

69
00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:25.180
The smallest degree is small delta of G

70
00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.100
here one, at f.

71
00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.006
Careful: the course summary calls small

72
00:03:31.097 --> 00:03:33.377
delta the maximal degree.

73
00:03:33.377 --> 00:03:34.835
That is a typo.

74
00:03:34.835 --> 00:03:37.388
Small delta is the minimum

75
00:03:37.388 --> 00:03:39.850
capital delta the maximum.

76
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.037
A graph is r regular when every

77
00:03:43.129 --> 00:03:45.783
vertex has the same degree r.

78
00:03:45.783 --> 00:03:48.254
This graph is not regular

79
00:03:48.254 --> 00:03:51.640
its degrees run from one up to four.

80
00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:54.546
Back to the prediction.

81
00:03:54.647 --> 00:03:58.187
Two plus three plus four plus three

82
00:03:58.288 --> 00:04:01.423
plus three plus one is sixteen.

83
00:04:01.423 --> 00:04:03.850
Not eight, but sixteen

84
00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:06.580
twice the number of edges.

85
00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.226
This is the handshake lemma.

86
00:04:09.226 --> 00:04:12.833
In every graph, the degrees add up to twice

87
00:04:12.915 --> 00:04:14.555
the number of edges.

88
00:04:14.637 --> 00:04:16.604
The proof counts one set

89
00:04:16.686 --> 00:04:18.490
in two different ways.

90
00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:22.226
The set we count is every pair made of

91
00:04:22.315 --> 00:04:25.523
a vertex and an edge at that vertex.

92
00:04:25.523 --> 00:04:28.909
Put a tick at each end of every edge.

93
00:04:28.909 --> 00:04:31.226
Counted vertex by vertex

94
00:04:31.226 --> 00:04:35.236
each vertex has as many ticks as its degree

95
00:04:35.236 --> 00:04:38.800
so the count is the sum of the degrees.

96
00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:40.878
Counted edge by edge

97
00:04:40.878 --> 00:04:43.922
each edge has exactly two endpoints

98
00:04:43.922 --> 00:04:47.049
because an edge is a two-element set.

99
00:04:47.049 --> 00:04:49.764
So every edge carries two ticks

100
00:04:49.847 --> 00:04:51.986
and the count is two times

101
00:04:52.068 --> 00:04:53.714
the number of edges.

102
00:04:53.714 --> 00:04:55.442
One set, two counts

103
00:04:55.442 --> 00:04:57.910
so the two numbers are equal.

104
00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:01.496
A common slip is to say that the degrees

105
00:05:01.571 --> 00:05:03.825
add up to the number of edges.

106
00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:06.154
But every edge is counted once

107
00:05:06.229 --> 00:05:08.033
at each of its two ends.

108
00:05:08.033 --> 00:05:10.437
The edge a b counts once for a

109
00:05:10.437 --> 00:05:12.090
and once again for b.

110
00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:17.024
One consequence: the number of vertices with

111
00:05:17.123 --> 00:05:19.695
odd degree is always even.

112
00:05:19.695 --> 00:05:23.950
Here they are b, d, e and f: four of them.

113
00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:24.622
Why?

114
00:05:24.622 --> 00:05:28.159
The total is even, and the even degrees add

115
00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:29.928
up to an even number.

116
00:05:30.008 --> 00:05:32.179
So the odd degrees must add

117
00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.270
up to an even number too

118
00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:37.727
and a sum of odd numbers is even only when

119
00:05:37.807 --> 00:05:40.380
there is an even number of them.

120
00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:44.281
So no graph has the degrees three, three

121
00:05:44.281 --> 00:05:45.753
three, one, one.

122
00:05:45.753 --> 00:05:48.957
They add up to eleven, which is odd.

123
00:05:49.044 --> 00:05:51.988
And no graph on seven vertices has

124
00:05:52.075 --> 00:05:54.499
every degree equal to three

125
00:05:54.499 --> 00:05:58.050
because seven times three is twenty-one.

126
00:05:58.630 --> 00:05:59.421
To sum up.

127
00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:02.425
A graph is a set of vertices together

128
00:06:02.504 --> 00:06:04.085
with a set of edges

129
00:06:04.085 --> 00:06:07.011
and each edge is a pair of vertices.

130
00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:09.382
The degree of a vertex counts

131
00:06:09.461 --> 00:06:10.726
the edges at it.

132
00:06:10.805 --> 00:06:13.335
And the degrees always add up to

133
00:06:13.414 --> 00:06:15.470
twice the number of edges.

134
00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.719
Try this yourself

135
00:06:17.719 --> 00:06:21.707
can a graph have the degrees three, three

136
00:06:21.707 --> 00:06:22.635
one, one?

137
00:06:22.635 --> 00:06:24.211
The sum is even

138
00:06:24.211 --> 00:06:27.364
so the handshake lemma allows it.

139
00:06:27.364 --> 00:06:28.570
Look closer.

140
00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:31.092
In part two: subgraphs

141
00:06:31.092 --> 00:06:33.924
cliques and independent sets

142
00:06:33.924 --> 00:06:37.040
and the answer to the timetable.
